Z||000000||count_iblis||11-13-2000||08:11 PM||||As you all know or have been following my post I have been debating on several engine options.  Well after hours of comteplating on wether to go with a used 2.8 or a 3.1 rebuild or a dropped 3.4 I have decided to go with the dropped 3.4.  Now I have found one for 800.00 dollars and my mechanic said he would install everything with the price of the engine for 1500.00...  Is this a good price? Also he was telling me that I need the wiring and the computer for the 3.4 alos.  Is this true?  I think this sounds like a good idea but I just want your guys input.  <P>------------------<BR>COUNTIBLIS <BR>"HUG YOUR KIDS SPANK THE SOUND BARRIER"||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||11-13-2000||08:16 PM||||I believe you may have to get a different ECM for the 3.4  Some may use the PassKey system, where you have to have that little thingy on the ignition key send a signal to the ECM for the engine to work.  I remember hearing something about this, Im not sure if it was for a 3.4 or a different engine, but if your mechanic says that you need a differnt ECM, that could be one of the reasons.<P>------------------<BR>"The fish who keeps on swimming is the first to chill upstream"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||11-13-2000||09:54 PM||||If you are talking about a 3.4 TDC, yes you need the ecm and wiring from the donor car. If it is a 3.4 pushrod with iron heads(Camaro/Firebird)you will use your Fiero intake system and all electronics. You will also have to relocate the starter to the opposite side of the engine. If it is the new front wheel drive 3.4 with aluminum heads(Grand Damn)you will need the ECM and electronics. All information mentioned is subject to change because I only have first hand experience with the iron head 3.4- Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||count_iblis||11-13-2000||10:25 PM||||Im going to get the a 94 3.4 out of a camaro.  so do i need the computer and the wiring?<P>------------------<BR>COUNTIBLIS <BR>"HUG YOUR KIDS SPANK THE SOUND BARRIER"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||11-13-2000||10:52 PM||||No, you shouldnt need the ECM. Everything from the heads up will go from the Fiero engine to the new 3.4. I think that is a good price for all the work. It can be done fairly easily yourself, but if you insist on a mechanic, then I think that is a good price.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig2.gif">     <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||count_iblis||11-13-2000||11:02 PM||||hey sean will i need the wiring also?  So your saying i dont need the computer then?  I can use my 2.8 computer and all?  I just need the block right?  Is that engine out of the 94 camaro the dohc?  how much hp and torque does it have?<P><P>------------------<BR>COUNTIBLIS <BR>"HUG YOUR KIDS SPANK THE SOUND BARRIER"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||11-14-2000||02:02 AM||||The 3.4 out of a camero is single cam. You will just be using the short block. All other parts will come off the Fiero 2.8. No added wires, no ECM, no special sensors. It would be a good Idea to have yours heads reworked while everything is apart. Just get the engine and tell your mech to swap the outside items and move the starter. As for HP I have only heard from various people with Fiero heads/intake it's about 180 HP. I think in camero form it's 160 hp. (The Fiero heads/intake are suppose to flow better than stock camero)||||reg||1
Z||000007||count_iblis||11-14-2000||02:12 AM||||thanks orief you just might have saved me some hundred dollars!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/count-sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||11-14-2000||02:16 AM||||The 3.4 heads are 100% identical to the 2.8 heads, so if the 3.4 comes complete you won't have to deal with swapping heads or valve jobs.<P>As stated above, if you use a Camaro/Firebird 3.4 you only need to drill new starter holes.  The stock Fiero wiring and ECM are retained.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||11-14-2000||07:57 AM||||Count,<P>Just copy the 3.4 page from Ed Park's home page: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <P>The article will tell you and yor mechanic what is needed.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000010||Shaun41178||11-14-2000||04:29 PM||||The 3.4 if transplanted into a fiero will not make 180 hp.  Here is my reasoning for this.<P>(1)The camshaft in the 3.4 Camaro engine has a less lift and less duration then the stock 2.8 Fiero cam.  This means nothing in itself but because of #2 read below<P>(2) The intake on the 3.4 camaro engine flows much better then the stock Fiero intake.  The runners on the 3.4 are larger and so is the throttle body. <P>With a smaller cam then a stock fiero and then putting smaller runners and throttle body on a 3.4, how could it possibly make more power then a camaro 3.4?  It can't is what it boils down to.  I think people pull their worn down 2.8 engines and install a semi fresh 3.4 or a new 3.4 and mistake the extra torque and the 20 extra factory rated hp as being in the 180-190 hp range.  But in all actuality it is making less hp then 160 because of the smaller intake runners and TB.  It might not be making less then 160 hp as the Fiero prom might be more aggressive but it is not making more then 160.  Mods such as a cam and headers and intake mods will be needed to get it up to the 180 hp range.<P><P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||9
Z||000011||zeusrekning||11-14-2000||05:54 PM||||Man you just F^@$ed all my planns up. I hope some one can show some good facts on what a 3.4 fiero will do. I've been planning on ding this and now i hear this. I will use the fiero intake no matter what though, just for looks and thought.<BR>Zeusrekning walks away crushed and cussing <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000012||Cooter||11-14-2000||09:39 PM||||Sorry to disagree with Shaun, but when I was getting my 3.4 ready to install, I was not going to use the Fiero intake, but the 3.4 instead. I even had it cut and welded to clear the distributer- that section of the intake acts like a balance pipe between the banks and does not hurt/help air flow. I found out by laying the intake components side by side that the port sizes are exactly the same size. The same upper intake gasket kit is used for both engines. Even the throttle body diameter was the same- remember the Fieros used a bigger bore than other 2.8's did. I finally just used the Fiero stuff because the Camaro doesnt have a hook up for the cold start injector and some other temp sensors. The Fiero intake has a greater volume and less obstruction to air flow than the Camaro's split bank intake.<BR>If you still want to use a camaro intake, you can have the one that I had modified to clear the distributer.||||reg||1
